      ;; The pair is checked before parking: an error raised\\n                       ;; after park-continuation would strand a continuation\\n                       ;; in :parked and consume an id counter.\\n                       {:keys [call-in]}\\n                       (ffi/require-call-pair! (:store vm) ffi-op)\\n6:   - **The host supplies streams.** `:stream/make` calls the composition's\\n7-     `:make-stream`; there is no transport identity in this namespace. Every\\n8-     other stream handler is a protocol call on a handle already in the store.\\n9-   - **Cursors are opaque.** A store cursor entry is\\n10-     `{:stream-id id :cursor <opaque>}`. Nothing increments one.\\n11-   - **Outcomes are maps, and every handler is total over its closed set.**\\n12-     `blocked` and `full` park; `end` and `gap` are values the program sees;\\n13-     the rest are errors that name their outcome.\\n14-   - **Waiters are gone.** No v2 transport is waitable, so the polling wait\\n15-     set is the mechanism rather than a fallback. Cadence comes from the\\n16-     driver above the VM.\\n17-   - **`:stream/take` is gone.** Destructive read needs a reader position in\\n18-     the medium, which the contract retired.\\n19-   - **The module registry is a value** carried in VM state, so effect\\n20-     dispatch and `resolve-var` read a supplied registry rather than a global.\\n21-   - **Program observation is not engine work.** `ready-for-ingress?` lives\\n22-     here because it speaks the scheduler's own vocabulary, but the program\\n23-     handle, cursor, and gap count belong to `dao.stream.observer`;\\n24-     nothing in this namespace polls a program stream.\\\"\\n25-  (:refer-clojure :exclude [gensym])\\n26-  (:require [clojure.set]\\n27-            [dao.datom :as datom]\\n28-            [dao.runtime :as rt]\\n29-            [dao.stream :as stream]\\n30-            [yin.vm :as vm]\\n31-            [yin.vm.module :as module]\\n32-            [yin.vm.runtime-adapter :as