e` and `transport-error` are errors that\\n178-   name their outcome, as v1's throw on a closed stream did.\\\"\\n179-  [state effect]\\n180-  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\\n181-        val (:val effect)\\n182-        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\\n183-        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\\n184-    (when (nil? handle) (fail \\\"Invalid stream reference\\\" {:ref stream-ref}))\\n185-    (let [result (stream/append! handle val)\\n186-          o (outcome result)]\\n187-      (case o\\n188-        :dao.stream/ok {:value val, :state state}\\n189-        :dao.stream/full {:park true, :stream-id stream-id, :state state}\\n190-        (fail \\\"Stream append failed\\\" {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id})))))\\n191-\\n192-\\n193-(defn handle-cursor\\n194:  \\\"Handle :stream/cursor. Mints an opaque cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`.\\n195-\\n196-   v1 fabricated `{:position 0}` and touched no stream. Minting is a stream\\n197-   operation, so `closed` and `transport-error` arrive here.\\n198-   Returns [cursor-ref updated-state].\\\"\\n199-  [state effect id]\\n200-  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\\n201-        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\\n202-        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\\n203-    (when (nil? handle) (fail \\\"Invalid stream reference\\\" {:ref stream-ref}))\\n204:    (let [cursor (vm/mint-oldest handle :stream/cursor)\\n205-          cursor-data (vm/cursor-entry stream-id cursor)\\n206-          new-store (assoc (:store state) id cursor-data)\\n207-          cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id id}]\\n208-      [cursor-ref (assoc state :store new-store)])))\\n209-\\n210-\\n211-(defn handle-next\\n212-  \\\"Handle :stream/next. Total over the seven read outcomes.\\n213-\\n214-   `ok` advances the store cursor to the exact returned successor. `blocked`\\n215-   parks. `end` yields nil, as v1 did. `gap` advances to the recovery cursor\\n216-   and yields `:dao.stream/gap`, so a program that reads it learns values were\\n217-   lost. The three ter