{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '91,128p'\nnl -ba src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream_v2.cljs | sed -n '587,610p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '91,128p'\nnl -ba src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream_v2.cljs | sed -n '587,610p'\"","aggregated_output":"    91\tvalue, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files, timers and windows\n    92\tall deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is what they call. The\n    93\thost library is handed the map. Each entry takes the host's arguments and\n    94\treturns one value; the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry\n    95\tcannot return a value to the host and cannot invoke application code. The\n    96\tadapter is handed only the deposit operation and its own host resource. If its\n    97\tdeposit channel fails, it may close that resource to make the boundary\n    98\tobservably gone; this is teardown, not an upward callback. Host types are\n    99\tclassified into plain data here -- a transform is the last place holding a host\n   100\terror object, and no host type may cross onto the stream.\n   101\t\n   102\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n   103\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n   104\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read, and it does\n   105\tnot need to. This is what keeps the layering one-way: a boundary depends on\n   106\tnothing above it, because whoever constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n   107\t\n   108\tA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n   109\tnone of those events semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol\n   110\tvalidation happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport\n   111\tmachinery, not an event the adapter is obliged to deposit. Once the transport\n   112\thas produced a valid host event, data is syntax (axiom 2), so worth is not a\n   113\tproperty of the datum -- an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to\n   114\tanother that does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys\n   115\tthe perspectives that have not been taken. How much is retained is the medium's\n   116\tretention policy, which is operational; what an event means is interpretation.\n   117\tNeither is the depositor's decision.\n   118\t\n   119\tAn adapter that takes a function to invoke has relocated the callback, not\n   120\tremoved it, even when every value it passes is plain data. An adapter whose\n   121\tcallback has a meaningful return value is worse: that is a synchronous call in\n   122\tboth directions wearing the shape of an event.\n   123\t\n   124\tHost events are ordinary values on an ordinary stream. A transport may use a\n   125\tqualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and additive evolution;\n   126\tthat envelope remains plain data and creates no separate event mechanism. A\n   127\tcomposition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary for facts that it\n   128\towns. Neither requires a bespoke bus or callback API.\n   587\t\n   588\t\n   589\t(defn main-view\n   590\t  []\n   591\t  (let [session (make-session)\n   592\t        emit #(emit! session %)]\n   593\t    (r/create-class\n   594\t      {:display-name \"continuation-stream-v2-main\",\n   595\t       :component-did-mount (fn [] (emit {:event :compile})),\n   596\t       :component-will-unmount (fn [] (emit {:event :unmount})),\n   597\t       :reagent-render\n   598\t       (fn []\n   599\t         (let [state @(:state session)\n   600\t               {:keys [source-code datoms k-stream cursors steps handoffs owner\n   601\t                       completed? result error vm-a vm-b]}\n   602\t               state\n   603\t               program-view\n   604\t               (if datoms\n   605\t                 (pretty-print {:code-datoms (count datoms),\n   606\t                                :datoms (take 40 datoms)})\n   607\t                 \"Compile source to generate code datoms.\")\n   608\t               queue-view (pretty-print (ct/in-flight-summary k-stream cursors))\n   609\t               stream-view\n   610\t               (pretty-print\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"The handoff suite passes: 9 tests, 57 assertions. The stream-resource and data-collision defects are resolved, and mutable state is now local to each view. One invariant issue remains: host callbacks call `emit!`, which appends the event and immediately executes the application interpreter. The architecture requires the host adapter to deposit the event and return."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-13 19:03:59 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-14 02:03:59 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Changes required; unconditional architecture sign-off withheld.**\n\n| severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P2 | [continuation_stream_v2.cljs:403](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream_v2.cljs:403) | **No callbacks / host boundary separation:** host callbacks invoke `emit!`, which deposits data but then synchronously calls `handle-event` and `schedule!`. The foundational rules require adapters to append and return without invoking application code. | Separate event deposition from consumption. Give host adapters only the deposit operation; let an independently driven session interpreter consume events, update state, and emit scheduling effects. |\n\nThe three original defects are resolved:\n\n- Stream resources block `shippable?` and cause serialization to reject shipment.\n- Escaped primitive/quote tags preserve nested literal maps and actual primitive references.\n- Mutable state and animation ownership are local to each mounted session.\n\n**Validation:** 9 JVM tests, 57 assertions passed. Browser/CLJS execution was not verified. No files changed."}}
